 # Regularization and Loss Functions
 
-Also called: NeRF Regularization, Training Objectives, Auxiliary Losses
 
 ## Overview
-Regularization and Loss Functions define the objective landscape for optimizing Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs), extending the standard photometric reconstruction loss to address specific failure modes such as overfitting in sparse views, geometric ambiguity ("floaters"), semantic incoherence, and style mismatches [S1, 8582-regnerf.md, 8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md]. This family sets itself apart from general optimization procedures by providing specialized mathematical formulations that constrain the density $\sigma$ and color $\mathbf{c}$ fields, or the rendered outputs, to adhere to prior knowledge (geometric, semantic, or statistical) rather than relying solely on pixel-wise alignment with ground truth images [S1, 8582-regnerf.md, 8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md].
+Regularization and Loss Functions refers to the family of mathematical terms added to the total loss function in Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) training to address specific artifacts, such as misalignment, distributed computation errors, or sparse view inconsistencies [S1, 7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md, 8582-regnerf.md]. It solves the problem where the standard photometric Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss is insufficient for handling scene motion, multi-GPU synchronization, or limited input data [S1]. This family sets itself apart from general regularization by providing specialized mathematical formulations for distinct failure modes, such as patch-based comparison for misalignment [8701-alignerf-training-objective.md] or cross-GPU aggregation for distortion [10377-nerf-xl-multi-gpu-distortion-loss.md].
 
